<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahuas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nahua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nahuas are a group of Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> who speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. In the late pre-Hispanic period they included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> and many other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of central Mexico. They remain the largest Indigenous group in modern Mexico.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahuas.jpg" alt="Nahuas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando Rosales · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ni%C3%B1as_Nahuas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Besides the Purépecha, whose language is unrelated to any other in Mesoamerica, the kingdom included <b>Nahua</b>, Otomí, Matlatzinca and other peoples, who kept their own languages but owed tribute and, in war, warriors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their settlements grew together into a single sprawling capital spread over hilltops, terraces and valley floor, and the confederation’s territory of some 1,400 square kilometres included Otomí as well as <b>Nahua</b> towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For the Aztecs, Tollan was the model of civilisation: in Nahuatl a toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’, meant art and refinement, while the <b>Nahua</b> informants of Bernardino de Sahagún credited the Toltecs with inventing medicine and the calendar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franciscan Bernardino de Sahagún worked for decades with <b>Nahua</b> scholars to compile the Florentine Codex, completed in the 1570s, a twelve-book encyclopaedia of Aztec gods, rituals, beliefs and society, written in Nahuatl and Spanish and illustrated by Nahua artists; it remains the most important source for Aztec…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the conquest, the <b>Nahuas</b> recorded their history, tribute, calendars and rituals in painted books, the codices, in a pictorial system that combined images with glyphs for names, places and dates.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Huexotzinco (modern Huejotzingo) was settled by Chichimec and <b>Nahua</b> groups in the Puebla valley in the 12th or 13th century, and grew into a powerful city-state that at times dominated neighbours such as Tlaxcala and Cholula.</blockquote><a href="/state/huexotzinco">Huexotzinco</a></li></ul></section>
